Key requirements for `-r` / `--recursive`:
- A directory operand is traversed in pre-order
- Within each directory, visit immediate entry names in ascending raw-byte order
- Search regular files, descend into directories, skip every symbolic link encountered during traversal
- An explicitly named symlink remains followed (i.e., if you pass a symlink directly as an operand, follow it)
- Avoid doubled separators for a trailing-slash operand
- Traversal must be deterministic and terminate safely on deep or resource-limited trees
- Diagnose unreadable entries and continue where possible

Prefix rules with `-r`:
- Without `-H` or `-h`, recursive search prefixes when there are multiple operands OR when at least one operand is a directory
- A single regular-file operand remains unprefixed
- The prefix is the deterministic path derived from the operand spelling and relative entry components
- `-h` suppresses it and `-H` forces it
- A directory without `-r` remains an error

